{
  "dom_id": "question-134230",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Leonard gave his dog Toby $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the puppy food he bought. What **exact** amount of food did Toby eat?\n\n*Remember that Leonard bought $\\frac{11}{7}$ kilograms of puppy dog food.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Toby ate$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$kilograms of food.",
  "worked_solution": "We know that Leonard bought $\\frac{11}{7}$ of a kilogram of puppy dog food.\n\nTo find the fraction of a kilogram that Toby ate, we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{4}$ by $\\frac{11}{7}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{1\\times 11}{4\\times 7}=\\frac{11}{28}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Toby ate $\\frac{11}{28}$ kilograms of food.",
